(f) Method and Output

Method:
Our group will develop a Python-based booking handler simulator to
investigate the performance differences between processes and
threads when handling multiple booking requests.
We will use the Azure Functions 2021 trace dataset as our
source of request execution durations. Each function invocation 
will be treated as a simulated booking request.
The experiment will involve three approaches:

1. Single-threaded execution: Requests will be processed one at
a time to establish a baseline.
2. Multithreading: Multiple booking requests will be handled 
concurrently using Python's ThreadPoolExecutor.
3. Multiprocessing: Multiple booking requests will be handled
simultaneously using Python's ProcessPoolExecutor.

We will use Python's standard libraries, including 
"concurrent.futures" and "time", to implement and
measure the different approaches. The "psutil" library will
be used to monitor CPU and memory usage, while Matplotlib will
be used to generate graphs.
Each approach will be tested using the same number of requests
and different worker configurations. We will repeat the 
experiments to improve the reliability of our results.

Output:

The project will produce:

- A functional Python booking handler simulator.
- A CSV file containing the benchmark results.
- Tables comparing execution time, throughput,
CPU usage and memory consumption.
- Graphs illustrating the performance differences
between single-threading, multithreading and 
multiprocessing.
- A final comparison identifying which approach
performs better under the tested conditions.

All experiments will be conducted locally on a
typical student laptop using free software,
without requiring paid services or specialised
hardware.
